    "regression_of": "2026-09-27, second failure, found by this spec on its first CI run after passing locally: pandoc 3.9 reads a Word figure into <figure> with a <figcaption>, and pandoc 3.1.3, which is what `apt-get install pandoc` gives CI on Ubuntu, writes a bare <img> and leaves the caption as an ordinary paragraph. The repair only knew the <figure> shape, so on CI's pandoc the image tag survived into the output. The assertion on the caption is written against its text rather than its italics for the same reason: which of the two the caption gets depends on the converter, and the text is the part that matters. 2026-09-27, first failure: converting us_spirits_distillation_column_study_guide.docx with pandoc alone produced 28 raw <table> blocks and 6 <figure> blocks. Word shades a single-cell table to draw a sidebar and GFM has no equivalent, so pandoc falls back to HTML, and the six <img> tags pointed at a media directory that was never extracted. Nothing failed: pandoc exited 0 and the .md looked plausible in a skim. Ingested, the chunker would have embedded the tags, and a retrieved chunk would have handed the judge angle brackets instead of the sidebar's text. The same document also arrived with no H1, because a Word title is a paragraph style rather than a heading level, so its ten sections were peer H1s with the title sitting above them as bold body text."
